		{"id":"9bfdc059e28f01fe","type":"file","file":"PhD-notes/Literature Review/2023 - Paper No 787 – Ammonia – Steam cascade heat pump for +100˚C steam generation - IEA.md","x":-6296,"y":1130,"width":400,"height":144,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0ee1b9d8aa0b76d7","type":"text","text":"# ~={cyan}Adsorption Heat Pumps=~\n\nhttps://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-72180-0_2\n\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1364032107000998\n\n## Why use Adsorption Heat pumps\n\n- Can operate with thermal driving energy sources such as waste heat, solar, and geothermal energies\n- Can work with low temperature driving energy sources\n- No moving parts \n- Low noise and vibration\n- Cam be employed as thermal energy storage device as the adsorbate stays in the adsorbent bed\n\nHowever\n\n- They have low COP values, \n- are intermittent, which means that cooling is only provided during certain phases of the cycle. This means that adsorption heat pumps are not applicable to use cases that needs continuous heating or cooling\n- Requires special design to maintain the vacuum and\n- Have a large volume and weight relative to traditional mechanical heat pump systems\n\nhttps://www.novapublishers.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/978-1-62417-070-6.pdf\n\nChemisorption heat pumps can reach up to $350 \\degree C$.","x":-5861,"y":981,"width":240,"height":81,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0c23e3bb76e58534","type":"text","text":"# ~={cyan}Mechanical Heat Pumps=~","x":-5786,"y":1172,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
